WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:04.330
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:11.860
We talked about a technique by the name Face2Face
back in 2016, approximately 300 videos ago.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:16.940
It was able to take a video of us and transfer
our gestures to a target subject.

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:22.030
With techniques like this, it’s now easier
and cheaper than ever to create these deepfake

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:27.190
videos of a target subject provided that we
have enough training data, which is almost

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:32.050
certainly the case for people who are the
most high-value targets for these kinds of

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:33.360
operations.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.360
Look here.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.940
Some of these videos are real, and some are
fake.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.230
What do you think, which is which?

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:46.760
Well, here are the results - this one contains
artifacts and is hence easy to spot, but the

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.730
rest…it’s tough.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:50.760
And it’s getting tougher by the day.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.260
How many did you get right?

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.970
Make sure to leave a comment below.

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:58.309
However, don’t despair, it’s not all doom
and gloom.

00:00:58.309 --> 00:01:05.329
Approximately a year ago, in came FaceForensics,
a paper that contains a large dataset of original

00:01:05.329 --> 00:01:07.310
and manipulated video pairs.

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:12.460
As this offered a ton of training data for
real and forged videos, it became possible

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:14.840
to train a deepfake detector.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:20.700
You can see it here in action as these green
to red colors showcase regions that the AI

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.270
correctly thinks were tampered with.

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:30.749
However, this followup paper by the name FaceForensics++
contains not only not only an improved dataset,

00:01:30.749 --> 00:01:36.369
but provides many more valuable insights to
help us detect these DeepFake videos, and

00:01:36.369 --> 00:01:37.369
even more.

00:01:37.369 --> 00:01:38.649
Let’s dive in.

00:01:38.649 --> 00:01:40.279
Key insight number one.

00:01:40.279 --> 00:01:44.679
As you’ve seen a minute ago, many of these
DeepFake AIs introduce imperfections, or in

00:01:44.679 --> 00:01:46.939
other words, artifacts to the video.

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:51.310
However, most videos that we watch on the
internet are compressed, and the compression

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:56.289
procedure…you have guessed right, also introduces
artifacts to the video.

00:01:56.289 --> 00:02:02.220
From this, it follows that hiding these DeepFake
artifacts behind compressed videos sounds

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:07.829
like a good strategy to fool humans and detector
neural networks likewise, and not only that,

00:02:07.829 --> 00:02:12.040
but the paper also shows us by how much exactly.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:17.519
Here you see a table where each row shows
the detection accuracy of previous techniques

00:02:17.519 --> 00:02:23.060
and a new proposed one, and the most interesting
part is how this accuracy drops when we go

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:30.630
from HQ to LQ, or in other words, from a high-quality
video to a lower-quality one with more compression

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:31.650
artifacts.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:37.870
Overall, we can get an 80-95% success rate,
which is absolutely amazing.

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:42.690
But, of course, you ask, amazing compared
to what?

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.069
Onwards to insight number two.

00:02:45.069 --> 00:02:51.409
This chart shows how humans fared in DeepFake
detection, as you can see, not too well.

00:02:51.409 --> 00:02:57.220
Don’t forget, the 50% line means that the
human guesses were as good as a coinflip,

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:59.819
which means that they were not doing well
at all.

00:02:59.819 --> 00:03:05.230
Face2face hovers around this ratio, and if
you look at NeuralTextures, you see that this

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:09.390
is a technique that is extremely effective
at fooling humans.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:11.640
And wait…what’s that?

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:16.450
For all the other techniques, we see that
the grey bars are shorter, meaning that it’s

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:21.569
more difficult to find out if a video is a
DeepFake because its own artifacts are hidden

00:03:21.569 --> 00:03:24.060
behind the compression artifacts.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:29.390
But the opposite is the case for NeuralTextures,
perhaps because its small footprint on the

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:30.390
videos.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:35.430
Note that a state of the art detector AI,
for instance, the one proposed in this paper

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:39.689
does way better than these 204 human participants.

00:03:39.689 --> 00:03:45.349
This work does not only introduce a dataset,
these cool insights, but also introduces a

00:03:45.349 --> 00:03:47.069
detector neural network.

00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:53.939
Now, hold on to your papers because this detection
pipeline is not only so powerful that it practically

00:03:53.939 --> 00:04:00.480
eats compressed DeepFakes for breakfast, but
it even tells us with remarkable accuracy

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.989
which method was used to tamper with the input
footage.

00:04:03.989 --> 00:04:04.989
Bravo!

00:04:04.989 --> 00:04:10.280
Now, it is of utmost importance that we let
the people know about the existence of these

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:13.230
techniques, this is what I am trying to accomplish
with this video.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:18.590
But that’s not enough, so I also went to
this year’s biggest NATO conference and

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:24.550
